# Rank 1513 / CVE-2024-25421: Openfire did not remove a deleted user's
# multi-user-chat affiliations, so a new user registering the same
# username inherited the old user's room privileges (privilege
# inheritance on identity reuse, CWE-269). MUCRoom in
# xmppserver/src/main/java/org/jivesoftware/openfire/muc/MUCRoom.java
# implemented GroupEventListener but had no UserEventListener: when a user
# deletion removes owner/admin/member/outcast affiliations in every room
# survived, and MultiUserChatManager's registration path matched the new
# account to them by bare JID. the fixing commit is the fixing change
# (confirmed, "OF-2795 / OF-2166: Remove MUC affiliation of a user that
# is being deleted. (#2420)"): MUCRoom now implements UserEventListener,
# and userDeleting() takes the chat-room lock, keeps the room owned by
# replacing a sole owner with an administrative account, then addNone()
# the deleted user's JID and syncs the room; MultiUserChatManager
# removes affiliations from rooms not loaded in memory, and
# LocalMUCRoomManager drops the deleted user's nickname entries. The task
# list carried no flag; the prefetch wave had marked the rank NO_COMMIT
# because a stale git lock from an earlier crash made the commit look
# unfetchable -- the commit resolves in the clone and is the fixing changex, so no

#
# ATTENTION, authorization-lifecycle vocabulary. The weakness is a
# missing revocation on an identity lifecycle event: no input flows
# anywhere, no operation is performed with attacker-controlled data, and
# the vulnerable class is simply silent where the fixed revision adds a handler.
# Nothing in the corpus can express "this affiliation store must react to
# user deletion" -- it is the same credential-versus-decision family as
# ranks 1501, 1504, 1505 and 1508, but one step more semantic: even the
# concept names are missing, not just the bindings. Measured on the real
# code: scanning the muc package at both revisions reports the same two
# VYQL-AUTH-079 rows (code.SensitiveOperation by auditreview.marks at
# MUCRoom.java:1803/1830 parent, 1806/1833 fix -- line shift only, the
# added imports), audit heuristics unrelated to the deletion path; the
# affiliation-lifecycle weakness is invisible at both. A binding on
# Openfire's own UserEventListener would detect this application rather
# than the weakness. No ENGINE-GAPS.md entry: the piece is ontology
# design (identity-lifecycle revocation), not a localised construct. No
# definition change shipped. Spec cve_rank1513 records a room without
# the deletion handler and the handler-bearing form, both asserting
# reject VYQL-AUTH-079.

